we actually see our Hello
World Coming up, right?

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:47.500
So now we actually have our
container that we wrote.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:49.660
That node.js, that simple
Hello World container.

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:51.247
We've containerized it.

00:10:51.247 --> 00:10:53.830
And we actually have Kubernetes
managing these containers now.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.300
And we have four
of them running.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:57.220
So far so good.

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:00.010
You know, looks like everything
is working pretty well.

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:00.760
It's replicated.

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:04.350
So if for whatever reason
a pod were to go down,

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:06.910
Kubernetes is going to keep
trying to get four running.

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:09.580
If you notice in that command
that I wrote up here earlier

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:12.939
by setting replicas to four
all I've really done here

00:11:12.939 --> 00:11:14.980
is-- you know, I didn't
mess with the config file

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:15.550
or anything like that.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:17.800
I just said, hey, I want
four replicas running.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.175
And Kubernetes are smart enough
to make sure that there's

00:11:20.175 --> 00:11:21.230
always four running.

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:23.410
We actually have a demo
upstairs called Whack-A-Pod

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.750
where, you know, we have
nine of these pods running.

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:28.340
And every time you keep trying
to whack one of the moles there

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:29.620
it's going to bring a pod down.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:31.960
But Kubernetes is going to
keep trying to keep all nine up

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:32.459
and running.

00:11:32.459 --> 00:11:34.150
So you can see how
fast Kubernetes

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:38.186
is at continually making
sure that your pods are

00:11:38.186 --> 00:11:38.810
up and running.

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:40.870
So here we have four running.

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:42.850
But let's say I want to
make a change, right?

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:45.390
I mean, you know, all of us
that are in software development

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:48.022
knows that, hey, code doesn't
sort of stay stagnant.

00:11:48.022 --> 00:11:49.480
So we're going to
go in here, we're

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:51.820
going to go server.js again.

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:54.530
This time, you know, we
really want to say, hey,

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:55.910
I don't really like Hello World.

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:58.243
