00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:04.120
that we had earlier.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:06.880
Now it created these new
pods that start with 968.

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:10.750
And slowly it's going
to start terminating

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:11.590
all of the old ones.

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:13.006
And all the traffic
will basically

00:13:13.006 --> 00:13:16.300
start moving to
these new 968 pods.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:17.770
If I sort of
continue with that we

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:20.860
see that, hey, within probably
about 30 seconds or so

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:22.060
it's done updating.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:25.670
And if I go back here again,
I should see Party People

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:29.439
if this demo works
correctly, and we do so.

00:13:29.439 --> 00:13:31.730
So you can see how like using
containers, and container

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:33.646
development, especially
with Container Engine,

00:13:33.646 --> 00:13:37.450
it's a lot quicker and easier
to manage your code in this way,

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:38.290
right?

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:40.930
Think about how you normally
go through a CI/CD type

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:44.800
of process, where maybe you
have to build with larger files.

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:47.290
And larger files have to get
pushed through the system.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:49.150
I mean, this is a way where
containers are fairly small.

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:49.858
They're portable.

00:13:49.858 --> 00:13:53.850
And you're able to get them
to the system fairly quickly.

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:56.790
So let's go back to
the slides right now.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:59.320
And I'll show you another
demo in a moment, as well.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:04.350
OK.

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:05.100
So the next thing.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.590
So what we did right now was
actually pretty easy, right?

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:09.780
I mean it wasn't too hard to do.

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:12.020
We were able to use a
lot of existing tools

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:14.070
to create a container image.

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:15.750
We push it up to the registry.

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:17.291
We're able to get
this stuff to work.

00:14:17.291 --> 00:14:18.870
But you know, what
about a lot of

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:22.300
the other common applications
that are out there, right?

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:25.930
So you know, let's
say you wanted to,

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:30.604
you know, WordPress, or
Redis, or MySQL, or Engine X.

00:14:30.604 --> 00:14:32.020
Like, you know,
you shouldn't have

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:35.620
to go through kind of the work
that we did to do that, right?

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:37.270
There's an open
community of people

00:14:37.270 --> 00:14:39.100
and Kubernetes is an
open source project.

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:40.780
So one of things we
definitely rely upon

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:42.880
is our open source
community to work together

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:46.720
to solve these types
of problems ourselves.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:49.964
So really what I want to do is,
you know, if you remember back

00:14:49.964 --> 00:14:51.880
to the days where you
were installing software

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:53.730
on a typical Linux box.

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:56.470
Maybe at first you
had to take a tarball.

00:14:56.470 --> 00:14:57.377
You had to un-tar it.

00:14:57.377 --> 00:14:58.460
You had to try to make it.

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:00.632
And you know, sometimes
you ran into errors.

00:15:00.632 --> 00:15:02.590
Like, you know, library
dependency type errors,

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:03.620
and things like that.

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:04.510
And it was really annoying.

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:06.218
And really, what kind
of helped with that

00:15:06.218 --> 00:15:07.630
was package managers, right?

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:10.046
Like YUM, and APT-Get, and
those kinds of things came out.

00:15:10.046 --> 00:15:12.280
And it made the installation
of software a lot easier

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:12.940
in those boxes.

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:15.922
Now, in Kubernetes the container
stuff that we sort of walked

00:15:15.922 --> 00:15:17.380
through earlier
was like, hey, this

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:19.129
is how you build a
container from scratch.

00:15:19.129 --> 00:15:21.670
But we wanted to build a
package manager as well.

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:23.892
So Helm is the package
manager for Kubernetes.

00:15:23.892 --> 00:15:26.350
And it really makes it easy
for you to start up, and build,

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:29.710
and run containers that are
most common in the marketplace,

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:30.940
right?

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:35.030
So this is an overview on Helm.

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:37.367
Helm runs a set of files
called Helm Charts.

00:15:37.367 --> 00:15:39.200
And they contain the
application definitions

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:41.600
that are necessary
for the Helm install.

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:43.249
So as I mentioned
earlier, Helm's

00:15:43.249 --> 00:15:44.540
the Kubernetes package manager.

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:46.456
Makes it very easy to
install many of the most

00:15:46.456 --> 00:15:47.540
common applications.

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:49.520
The list of generally
available helm charts,

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:51.650
we have over 50 today.

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:53.427
And those are the stable charts.

00:15:53.427 --> 00:15:55.010
We also have a lot
of incubator charts

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:56.190
that are still in progress.

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:59.600
So it's a growing list that
we've been talking about.

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:01.070
And you know,
specifically there's

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:02.528
one project that
we've been working

00:16:02.528 --> 00:16:06.200
on that's actually getting
ready for an alpha release

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:08.240
right now, right?

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:09.410
And this is SPARC, right?

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:12.020
So today users have had
to run big data workloads

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:14.030
on standalone SPARC
servers, or they

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:15.920
had to run SPARC
on yarn clusters,

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:18.050
or they had to run these
on Mesos clusters, right?

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:20.040
The community has been hard at
work with SPARC integration,

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:22.070
and Kubernetes is
giving users the option

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:24.920
to run their big data
jobs in Kubernetes, right?

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:28.320
So we had SPARC running in
standalone mode in Kubernetes

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:28.820
last year.

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:30.444
You can manually
launch a SPARC master.

00:16:30.444 --> 00:16:32.952
You can launch SPARC workers.

00:16:32.952 --> 00:16:34.160
And you can do these by hand.

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:35.750
But the architecture
didn't really

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:37.520
fully integrate with
Kubernetes for all

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:38.990
of the resource scheduling.

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:41.780
So the alpha that's
being ready to be

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:44.270
released right now directly
integrates with Kubernetes.

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:46.010
Launches executers
via a SPARC driver.

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:47.972
So it really scales up,
and it takes advantage

00:16:47.972 --> 00:16:49.430
of all the
orchestration management

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:51.050
that Kubernetes is
so good at doing.

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:53.510
So I mean, this is
a great first step

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:56.740
to enable big data SPARC
workloads to work on Kubernetes

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:58.700
and to let users of
big data move off

00:16:58.700 --> 00:17:01.790
of the isolated clusters that
you may have onto a shared

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:03.710
infrastructure with
the other applications

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:06.065
that you run in
Kubernetes, right?

00:17:06.065 --> 00:17:07.940
Ultimately, we want to
see new things enabled

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:09.481
by having big data
closely integrated

00:17:09.481 --> 00:17:12.177
with modern microservices-based
applications, all

00:17:12.177 --> 00:17:13.760
of these things
running in Kubernetes.

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:16.312
So the community
is hard at work.

00:17:16.312 --> 00:17:18.770
And the community that's largely
been responsible for SPARC

00:17:18.770 --> 00:17:20.210
at Kubernetes
includes developers

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:21.501
from a wide range of companies.

00:17:21.501 --> 00:17:23.750
I mean, there are developers
from Palantir, Google,

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:26.460
Pepperdata, Intel, Redhead,
Holloway, many others.

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:30.860
So this is, like I said,
it's alpha ready very soon.

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:32.630
If you're interested
in trying this out,

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:35.030
and if you're a company
that uses a lot of SPARC,

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:37.160
and you want to try running
SPARC on Kubernetes,

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:39.380
go to that link up there,
and try downloading it,

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:41.930
and play with it, because
this is really the way

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:43.940
that we start getting
applications running

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:46.695
within Kubernetes,
and in containers,

00:17:46.695 --> 00:17:48.320
and they eventually
become Helm charts,

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:51.594
and then the whole community
benefits from that.

00:17:51.594 --> 00:17:53.010
So why should you
use Helm, right?

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:54.660
So Helm is easy to
use because much

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:55.600
of the work in
getting the container

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:56.940
is already done for you, right?

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:00.870
So you should use Helm
because it's really fast.

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:01.570
It's convenient.

00:18:01.570 --> 00:18:02.220
It's reliable.

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:04.560
There's no reason to reinvent
the wheel when someone else is

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:06.390
in the community has already
done a lot of that work for,

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:07.110
you right?

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:09.480
And you know, because we are
a community, if you don't

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:11.910
see a Helm chart for a piece
of software that's fairly

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:13.820
commonplace, go build it.

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:14.850
Build one.

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:16.344
Contribute to the community.

00:18:16.344 --> 00:18:18.760
And now everyone else will
have that Helm chart available.

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:20.676
So we've been emphasizing
this whole community

00:18:20.676 --> 00:18:21.870
aspect of Helm a lot.

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:23.564
And just, I think,
three months ago

00:18:23.564 --> 00:18:25.980
there were only like 10 charts
out there that were stable.

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:26.980
And now there's over 50.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:29.502
So you know, it's
really worked, in terms

00:18:29.502 --> 00:18:30.960
of bringing this
community together

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:33.360
to help get containerized
applications up and running.

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:38.030
So let's take a quick look.

00:18:38.030 --> 00:18:40.970
I mean, we're going to
do a quick demo on Helm.

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:44.080
And I'll show you how quickly
and easily we can actually get

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:45.460
Helm up and running, all right?

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:53.000
Let me get the command up.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:54.230
So that's all Helm.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:56.810
I'm actually going to install
this by using Helm Redis.

00:18:56.810 --> 00:19:00.170
So we'll clear that up.

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:02.860
So this is basically the only
command that you need to run.

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:08.009
So hey, believe it or not,
like that one command, Helm,

00:19:08.009 --> 00:19:09.550
this command right
here, Helm install

