It's really wonderful.

00:21:00.790 --> 00:21:01.840
All it really does.

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:06.213
If I set a variable like x to
10 then I can increment it.

00:21:06.213 --> 00:21:08.840
You know, and keeping
incrementing it.

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:12.300
And, you know, if I
get x, you know, well--

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:14.450
so I get stuff like that.

00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:18.390
If I exit out of this stuff,
we have Redis up and running.

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:20.780
So essentially what I was
trying to prove to you

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:22.989
is that, hey, we have Redis
sort of up and running,

00:21:22.989 --> 00:21:25.280
and we have an actual instance
of Redis up and running.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:27.620
And instead of going
through a lot of the work

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:31.370
that we did earlier with
building the container, instead

00:21:31.370 --> 00:21:33.140
all I did here was I
did the Helm install.

00:21:33.140 --> 00:21:35.430
And that was really all it
took to get up and running.

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:37.804
So if you want to get started
with container development,

00:21:37.804 --> 00:21:39.750
yeah, take your
existing applications,

00:21:39.750 --> 00:21:42.530
you know, containerize them, and
run them in Container Engine.

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:45.560
But if you want to build
a WordPress instance,

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:49.460
for example, quickly just
do that do that in Helm.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:50.900
The various charts
are available.

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:52.400
We can just do a Helm search.

00:21:52.400 --> 00:21:55.770
And this list here are all
of the currently stable Helm

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:56.270
charts.

00:21:56.270 --> 00:21:58.520
So you can kind
of see that a lot

00:21:58.520 --> 00:22:00.050
of the sort of
typical applications

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:01.622
that you might
want are in there.

00:22:01.622 --> 00:22:03.080
Because the list
is getting really,

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:06.230
pretty big you can also
do a search, and say,

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:08.810
hey, I want anything that
has the keyboard Redis in it.

00:22:08.810 --> 00:22:10.810
And it'll give me the
stable charts for Redis.

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:14.482
So that's essentially
how we use Helm charts.

00:22:14.482 --> 00:22:16.940
I think we can move back to
the actual presence, the slides

00:22:16.940 --> 00:22:17.439
again.

00:22:19.892 --> 00:22:20.600
So that was Helm.

00:22:23.011 --> 00:22:24.510
So our first hello
world application

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:25.750
was pretty easy, right?

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:27.340
And using Helm Charts
was pretty easy.

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:29.050
It was pretty easy
to build a container.

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:30.550
We push it up to
container registry.

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:32.169
We ran it in Container Engine.

00:22:32.169 --> 00:22:34.210
You know, using Helm we
were able to containerize

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:35.510
a lot of the common
applications.

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:37.676
But as you start writing
more and more applications,

00:22:37.676 --> 00:22:39.310
and using containers,
your application

00:22:39.310 --> 00:22:40.810
will likely start
getting a lot more

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:43.090
complex than kind of the
examples I gave today, right?

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:44.590
Inevitably you'll
come across a need

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:46.131
where you need to
start sharing data.

00:22:46.131 --> 00:22:48.370
You need to mount volumes
across multiple pods.

00:22:48.370 --> 00:22:50.440
So this is where
persistent volumes comes.

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:53.320
And I did want to
talk through how

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:57.040
we use persistent
volumes in Kubernetes.

00:22:57.040 --> 00:22:58.930
So let's start with
identifying the problem

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:00.055
that we're trying to solve.

00:23:00.055 --> 00:23:03.490
So first, we have this basic
unit of scheduling Kubernetes,

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:04.540
which is a pod, right?

00:23:04.540 --> 00:23:06.910
So in Kubernetes, looking
at Kubernetes' concepts

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:10.870
for a second, we had containers
that we built earlier,

00:23:10.870 --> 00:23:13.810
and containers in Kubernetes
are organized in pods.

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:16.960
A pod consists of one or
more containers, right?

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:19.480
And typically when you have a
container it doesn't actually

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:21.861
share its file system
with anybody else, right?

00:23:21.861 --> 00:23:23.860
So things normally don't
show their file system,

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:26.970
but you have a pod that has
multiple containers in it.

00:23:26.970 --> 00:23:28.637
And when you start
organizing your pods,

00:23:28.637 --> 00:23:30.761
like, well, one of the
questions I do get sometimes

00:23:30.761 --> 00:23:33.100
is well, which containers
should be inside a pod?

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:34.510
And it kind of depends on you.

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:35.980
Like if you want to
start sharing things,

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:38.563
like, that's the same network,
you want to guarantee that it's

00:23:38.563 --> 00:23:41.780
going to be running on the same
node then, yeah, share these.

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:44.991
And some examples might be if
you have an Engine X container,

00:23:44.991 --> 00:23:46.865
and then if you're
running a PHP application,

00:23:46.865 --> 00:23:48.224
so you have a PHP container.

00:23:48.224 --> 00:23:50.140
Might want to combine
those two because you're

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:53.450
going to sort of scale them up
appropriately at the same time.

00:23:53.450 --> 00:23:57.262
But if you had, like, a database
container and a front end

00:23:57.262 --> 00:23:58.720
container, you may
not, because you

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:00.290
might want to scale your
databases differently

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:01.414
than your front end, right?

00:24:04.060 --> 00:24:05.560
So what that we've
looked at here

00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:08.200
is that pods are composed of
these multiple containers.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.990
But it would be really nice
if these different containers

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:13.390
within a pod could still
share the same value,

00:24:13.390 --> 00:24:15.820
you know, for speed, and for
a lot of different purposes,

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:16.360
right?

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:19.190
So the second thing is that
containers are ephemeral,

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:19.690
right?

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:21.940
Like we saw when we did
the replications over there

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:25.330
that we had four, and whenever
I updated code all it really did

00:24:25.330 --> 00:24:27.545
was brought up four new pods.

00:24:27.545 --> 00:24:29.170
And it just killed
the old ones, right?

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:33.790
So these pods are these
short-lived units of work.

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:35.880
And imagine if there's
like a piece of storage

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:36.880
attached to that, right?

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:39.469
So this means that if
that container crashed

00:24:39.469 --> 00:24:41.260
or the machine is
restarted then you end up

00:24:41.260 --> 00:24:42.551
losing all of your data, right?

00:24:42.551 --> 00:24:45.517
So you know, with the Hello
World app that we had,

00:24:45.517 --> 00:24:46.600
we didn't really use data.

00:24:46.600 --> 00:24:47.725
So it didn't really matter.

00:24:47.725 --> 00:24:49.720
But you know, for a
more complex application

00:24:49.720 --> 00:24:52.000
that uses data back and
forth, like this, can

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:52.770
be a big problem.

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:55.850
And you know, if you imagine
trying to run applications

00:24:55.850 --> 00:24:57.950
such as like a stateful
type of application,

00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:01.060
whether it be a database or
some other larger application

00:25:01.060 --> 00:25:04.240
like that, I mean, having
your data disappear

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:06.000
is just a nonstarter, right?

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:08.290
Like, it's not going to
work for what we want to do.

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:09.432
So those are the problems.

00:25:09.432 --> 00:25:11.640
And this is where Kubernetes
volumes comes in, right?

00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:14.250
So at its core a volume
is just a directory,

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.734
that you could pre-populate
some data in it.

00:25:16.734 --> 00:25:19.150
And that's accessible to all
the different containers that

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:20.920
are within a pod, right?

00:25:20.920 --> 00:25:24.040
And how that directory comes
to be in the medium that

00:25:24.040 --> 00:25:26.200
backs it up the
contents, what happens

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:27.880
after a pod's terminated?

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:30.010
Those are all sort of
implementation details

00:25:30.010 --> 00:25:31.280
that we'll talk about.

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:32.830
But it depends on
the type of volume.

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:34.246
And you can configure
all of that.

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:40.180
If we take a closer look
at this, like, Kubernetes,

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:42.160
the volume plugins can
be largely categorized

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:42.900
into two types.

00:25:42.900 --> 00:25:45.017
So the first type are
the persistent volumes.

00:25:45.017 --> 00:25:46.600
And, you know, there's
obviously a lot

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:48.766
of support for a lot of
different persistent volumes

00:25:48.766 --> 00:25:50.740
out there, right?

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:53.290
So these are storage
that outlives

00:25:53.290 --> 00:25:54.340
the lifecycle of a pod.

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:56.950
So in other words, it could
be anything from an NFS Share

00:25:56.950 --> 00:25:58.045
that you see up there.

00:25:58.045 --> 00:25:59.920
Could be a cloud provider
with virtual disks.

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:03.040
So lifecycle of storage is
independent from the lifecycle

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:03.790
of the pod, right?

00:26:03.790 --> 00:26:07.390
So if the pod dies the
storage is still alive.

00:26:07.390 --> 00:26:08.380
And so that's great.

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:10.960
So if a pod referencing one
of these volumes is created,

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:13.150
storage is made available
inside it, it's attached,

00:26:13.150 --> 00:26:14.500
it's mounted.

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:16.450
But then, when the
pod's terminated volumes

00:26:16.450 --> 00:26:17.930
are unmounted,
could be detached,

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:19.780
but the data's always
still there, right?

00:26:19.780 --> 00:26:21.130
So the key is the data persists.

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:24.244
So if another pod comes along,
references the same storage

00:26:24.244 --> 00:26:25.660
volume, with the
same mount point,

00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:28.160
then that storage will
still have the data there.

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:31.270
So it's pretty convenient to be
able to have a persistent data

00:26:31.270 --> 00:26:32.530
store, right?

00:26:32.530 --> 00:26:34.180
The ephemeral side
are what we've

00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:36.560
been talking about
till now, and is

00:26:36.560 --> 00:26:39.190
what's used in pods,
and in a lot of volumes

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:41.580
today, where as soon
as the pod's created,

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:42.610
you create this volume.

00:26:42.610 --> 00:26:44.360
But then when the pod's
deleted the volume

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:45.340
just disappears, right?

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:48.910
So it's usually a scratch
space, a shared space.

00:26:48.910 --> 00:26:54.220
And it's a space that could be
shared between the containers

00:26:54.220 --> 00:26:54.787
and a pod.

00:26:54.787 --> 00:26:55.870
But it's going to go away.

00:26:55.870 --> 00:26:57.849
So the type of data that
you would put in there

00:26:57.849 --> 00:26:59.140
is usually temporary type data.

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:04.720
So let's walk through an
example to help sort of clarify

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:05.814
a lot of this, right?

00:27:05.814 --> 00:27:06.730
So here we have a pod.

00:27:06.730 --> 00:27:09.880
Pod, as we said earlier,
the basic unit of scheduling

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:10.480
Kubernetes.

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:11.650
Contains one or more containers.

00:27:11.650 --> 00:27:13.525
And in this case, we
have a single container.

00:27:13.525 --> 00:27:15.310
We called it Sleepy
Container here.

00:27:15.310 --> 00:27:18.934
And that's the Google
Container Registry image there.

00:27:18.934 --> 00:27:21.100
It's just a container that
kind of sleeps, is really

00:27:21.100 --> 00:27:22.420
all it does.

00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:25.360
But if we look at how to set
up storage for this container,

00:27:25.360 --> 00:27:27.430
well, we configure
storage there, right?

00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:31.780
In this case we used a Google
Compute Engine persistent disk.

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:33.230
We called it Panda Disk.

00:27:33.230 --> 00:27:39.100
And what this file here assumes
is that disk has already

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:39.880
been procured.

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:42.810
It's already there.

00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:45.252
Supposing like that this disk
already exists someplace,

00:27:45.252 --> 00:27:46.960
and this is going to
reference it, right?

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:48.584
So it's referencing
it directly, right?

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:55.120
So then the pod also defines
where we should mount that disk

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:56.080
that we saw, right?

00:27:56.080 --> 00:27:58.670
So in this example, all
we did was just said,

00:27:58.670 --> 00:28:00.070
hey, we want to
mount that volume

00:28:00.070 --> 00:28:04.270
data that we see up there, that
Panda Disk, over to slash data.

00:28:04.270 --> 00:28:06.040
Seems pretty trivial.

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:08.730
But there's actually a lot
going on under the scenes.

00:28:09.310 --> 00:28:10.810
One of the concepts
with Kubernetes

00:28:10.810 --> 00:28:13.200
is you have these notes
that are running pods.

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:16.600
And so when your pod
is attached to a node,

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:19.340
then Kubernetes knows to
make an API call to say,

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:21.050
hey, where is this
persistent disk?

00:28:21.050 --> 00:28:23.650
It attaches that persistent
disk to that node, which

00:28:23.650 --> 00:28:25.270
then attaches it to your pod.

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:27.820
Now, one of the things
that Kubernetes also

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:30.040
can do very well, and is
a power of Kubernetes,

00:28:30.040 --> 00:28:33.424
is if a node is having issues
then it will create a new node,

00:28:33.424 --> 00:28:35.590
and it'll drain out that
node, and your pod will get

00:28:35.590 --> 00:28:37.090
moved to another node, right?

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:40.190
It automatically sort of
handles all that for you.

00:28:40.190 --> 00:28:43.900
So typically most
Kubernetes developers

00:28:43.900 --> 00:28:46.300
don't really care where
your containers are running.

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:47.716
As long as it's
running somewhere.

00:28:47.716 --> 00:28:52.060
So in that sense, your pod
might be moved to a new node.

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:54.730
And when it does that,
Kubernetes is smart enough

00:28:54.730 --> 00:28:57.534
to automatically
detect that change,

00:28:57.534 --> 00:28:59.950
detach this from the first
node that was having a problem,

00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:02.010
attach it to this other
node, and any time

00:29:02.010 --> 00:29:05.260
your pod sort of moves nodes
your volumes will always

00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:07.180
follow it and
Kubernetes always knows

00:29:07.180 --> 00:29:09.940
to make sure that it tracks
it, and attaches it to the node

00:29:09.940 --> 00:29:11.180
itself.

00:29:11.180 --> 00:29:14.115
So there's lots sort of going
on under the scenes in there.

00:29:14.115 --> 00:29:15.490
And Kubernetes
takes care of this

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:17.450
but, you know, I mean,
this is pretty cool,

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:18.824
and it works, but
we can actually

00:29:18.824 --> 00:29:20.816
do a lot better than this.

00:29:20.816 --> 00:29:22.690
And that's really what
persistent volumes is.

00:29:22.690 --> 00:29:25.030
Persistent volumes
is what Kubernetes

00:29:25.030 --> 00:29:26.346
does to manage volumes.

00:29:26.346 --> 00:29:28.720
And it's a little bit better
than what we just looked at.

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:31.030
Persistent volumes, it's
an abstract storage layer

00:29:31.030 --> 00:29:32.710
that is used and
managed by Kubernetes.

00:29:32.710 --> 00:29:36.680
So the way to describe this is
we have two different roles.

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:38.972
They could be the same person
within your organization.

00:29:38.972 --> 00:29:41.471
We have this first role that's
is cluster administrator that

00:29:41.471 --> 00:29:44.260
creates a number of persistent
volumes that are available.

00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:45.640
So the admin provisions them.

00:29:45.640 --> 00:29:48.400
And then we have users
that claim the volumes

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:49.910
that they want to use.

00:29:49.910 --> 00:29:52.240
So in some ways,
it's similar to how

00:29:52.240 --> 00:29:55.030
like an admin might create a
cluster with a bunch of nodes,

00:29:55.030 --> 00:29:58.390
and then users say, I want
replicas and pods running.

00:29:58.390 --> 00:29:59.860
And you know,
they'll automatically

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:01.150
get assigned to these nodes.

00:30:01.150 --> 00:30:03.190
So in that sense a
user says, I need,

00:30:03.190 --> 00:30:04.570
you know, say, 100 gigabytes.

00:30:04.570 --> 00:30:06.942
And if there's no gigabytes
available in any volume

00:30:06.942 --> 00:30:08.650
then it will get
assigned to that, right?

00:30:08.650 --> 00:30:12.700
So in static
provisioning, you know,

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:14.270
that's a fairly simple use case.

00:30:14.270 --> 00:30:18.080
And that if you make a claim
and there's a preview available

00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:19.259
it'll attach it to that.

00:30:19.259 --> 00:30:20.800
Dynamic provisioning,
which I'm going

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:22.720
to go into a little
more detail later,

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:24.110
works through a thing
called StorageClass.

00:30:24.110 --> 00:30:25.776
But that's actually
pretty cool because,

00:30:25.776 --> 00:30:27.310
hey, we all sort
of work and live

00:30:27.310 --> 00:30:29.194
in this world of
cloud now, right?

00:30:29.194 --> 00:30:30.610
Does it really
make a lot of sense

00:30:30.610 --> 00:30:33.040
to sort of pre-provision
storage that's available

00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:36.120
when, ideally what you
would do is a user says,

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:37.270
I need 100 gigs.

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:40.072
We don't say, hey, is there a
100-gig volume available for me

00:30:40.072 --> 00:30:41.030
to attach to this user?

00:30:41.030 --> 00:30:43.270
Instead you say,
well, OK, I'm going

00:30:43.270 --> 00:30:45.130
to go out and get
100 gigs for you now.

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:47.230
Find the storage for you
somewhere in the cloud.

00:30:47.230 --> 00:30:48.580
And then attach it, right?

00:30:48.580 --> 00:30:51.180
So that's where dynamic
provision comes in.

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:53.314
And it's a new sort
of unique feature

00:30:53.314 --> 00:30:54.355
that's within Kubernetes.

00:30:54.355 --> 00:30:56.680
You know we alpha
launched it in 1.2.

00:30:56.680 --> 00:30:58.540
We beta launched it at 1.4.

00:30:58.540 --> 00:30:59.980
We're ready to
make this generally

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:04.120
available with 1.6, which is
ready to launch in a few weeks.

00:31:04.120 --> 00:31:06.400
At the end of March is when
the 1.6 release date is.

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:08.840
And that's when dynamic
provisioning will be available

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:09.340
as well.

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:15.100
So let's walk through
this a little bit.

00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:17.490
So this is a simple case of
static provision and release.

00:31:17.490 --> 00:31:20.190
So we start here with the
cluster administrator.

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:23.760
This is what a yaml file looks
like for the persistent volume.

00:31:23.760 --> 00:31:25.350
You know, we see
here that there's two

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:26.349
that they have assigned.

00:31:26.349 --> 00:31:29.385
One is a 10-gig volume there.

00:31:29.385 --> 00:31:31.260
And then we have another
one that's 100 gigs.

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:34.170
So the cluster
administrator said, hey,

00:31:34.170 --> 00:31:36.960
these are the persistent
volumes that I have available.

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:39.780
There's two for you, right?

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:42.450
And if you run that kube
control create command

00:31:42.450 --> 00:31:44.730
on that yaml file, and then
you run a get PV it says,

00:31:44.730 --> 00:31:46.770
hey, these are the two
statuses available.

00:31:46.770 --> 00:31:49.900
So they're not actually
assigned to anybody.

00:31:49.900 --> 00:31:51.410
So you know, pretty
straightforward.

00:31:51.410 --> 00:31:53.510
Someone basically
said, hey, I have

00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:57.880
these two volumes available
for anyone to use, right?

00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:00.610
So in other words, the
cluster administrator

00:32:00.610 --> 00:32:04.270
creates a persistent value, and
makes some available for use.

00:32:04.270 --> 00:32:06.330
Now we have this user
that comes along.

00:32:06.330 --> 00:32:09.420
And the user creates what we
call a persistent volume claim,

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:09.990
right?

00:32:09.990 --> 00:32:13.010
This user is saying, hey I
have a persistent volume claim

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:14.370
that I'd like to have.

00:32:14.370 --> 00:32:15.920
I need 100 gigs of storage.

00:32:15.920 --> 00:32:17.845
So this is the claim
that they're making.

00:32:17.845 --> 00:32:19.470
And so we have a
couple of things here.

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:22.260
We have the persistent volume
that was assigned to that yaml.

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:24.135
Here we have a persistent
volume claim that's

00:32:24.135 --> 00:32:26.460
being made against that, right?

00:32:26.460 --> 00:32:30.440
So the user creates a
persistent volume claim.

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:33.082
And now if we take a step back
and look at what happened,

00:32:33.082 --> 00:32:34.790
well, first we ran
the persistent volume,

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:36.620
and created the two
volumes that we saw.

00:32:36.620 --> 00:32:39.380
Next we created the
persistent volume claim.

00:32:39.380 --> 00:32:41.840
And now if we do a
kube control get PVs

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:44.730
we see that, hey, you know
the claim was for 100 gigs.

00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:48.380
So we see that the 100 gig
that was created is now bound

00:32:48.380 --> 00:32:50.480
and it's claimed by
that claim, which

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:53.180
is the test that has my PVC.

00:32:53.180 --> 00:32:57.650
So here we've statically
bound the claim together.

00:32:57.650 --> 00:33:01.130
So if another user comes along
with another claim for 100 gigs

00:33:01.130 --> 00:33:05.040
this is not available to that
user at that point, right?

00:33:05.040 --> 00:33:08.270
So the binder works to
actually make that match.

00:33:08.270 --> 00:33:11.650
And you know, it's fairly
straightforward in this case.

00:33:11.650 --> 00:33:13.400
Like they need 100
gig, and there happened

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:14.441
to be 100 gigs available.

00:33:14.441 --> 00:33:17.484
So it was made available there.

00:33:17.484 --> 00:33:19.400
So now we end up sort
of with this happy state

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:23.240
where the user then creates
a pod and in the pod

00:33:23.240 --> 00:33:25.520
they say which claim they want.

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:29.606
And I think I have an example
of that yaml file as well.

00:33:29.606 --> 00:33:30.980
And this is what
that looks like.

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:34.310
So the pod that we had
before, if you remember,

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:37.100
it's had that GCE persistent
disk, that Panda disk.

00:33:37.100 --> 00:33:39.920
So at that point we had
to make this assumption

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:42.621
that Panda disk already exists.

00:33:42.621 --> 00:33:44.120
And that it's already
been procured.

00:33:44.120 --> 00:33:45.710
And it's already been set up.

00:33:45.710 --> 00:33:47.364
We've abstracted this
now to say, well,

00:33:47.364 --> 00:33:49.280
we don't have to make
that assumption anymore.

00:33:49.280 --> 00:33:52.890
All we're saying is that we
have this PV claim that we want,

00:33:52.890 --> 00:33:55.040
and so we know that in
this other yaml file

00:33:55.040 --> 00:33:57.740
we had identified the
PV claim to be 100 gigs.

00:33:57.740 --> 00:34:00.110
And so all we're saying
here is I need that.

00:34:00.110 --> 00:34:01.190
I reference a claim.

00:34:01.190 --> 00:34:03.830
So this pod is now
referencing the claim itself.

00:34:03.830 --> 00:34:06.200
And it's no longer
referencing the actual sort

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:09.389
of mounted piece of persistent
disk that we needed.

00:34:09.389 --> 00:34:11.389
So this is a lot better
than what we had before,

00:34:11.389 --> 00:34:13.190
because now we've sort
of abstracted this out.

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:15.409
You can take this file,
move it to any other server,

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:17.870
and as long as you
reference the claim then it

00:34:17.870 --> 00:34:19.520
all it'll just find a disk.

00:34:19.520 --> 00:34:22.070
If Panda disk were to
go away and get deleted,

00:34:22.070 --> 00:34:24.049
like all your pods
that are referencing

00:34:24.049 --> 00:34:25.340
it won't start breaking, right?

00:34:25.340 --> 00:34:25.881
In this case.

00:34:25.881 --> 00:34:30.710
Because the claim can get
moved from one PV to another PV

00:34:30.710 --> 00:34:33.449
fairly easily.

00:34:33.449 --> 00:34:35.659
So what we kind
of saw here was we

00:34:35.659 --> 00:34:37.429
have a pod that
referenced that claim.

00:34:37.429 --> 00:34:39.960
The name is also tied to
the persistent volume.

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:41.510
And so the example
here is, let's say

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:43.170
the pod writes a piece of data.

00:34:43.170 --> 00:34:45.260
So now we have that
little star in that folder

00:34:45.260 --> 00:34:47.690
up there because we have
a specific piece of data

00:34:47.690 --> 00:34:51.500
that was written by that pod
through the persistent volume

00:34:51.500 --> 00:34:53.591
claim to the persistent
volume, right?

00:34:53.591 --> 00:34:55.340
But now let's get a
little bit interesting

00:34:55.340 --> 00:34:58.120
here where the user says,
well, I want to delete the pod.

00:34:58.120 --> 00:34:59.760
I don't want the pod anymore.

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:02.240
So if the user goes out and
actually deletes this pod,

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:03.800
well, what happens, right?

00:35:03.800 --> 00:35:07.640
Well, really, I mean, nothing
really happens in a sense,

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:11.000
because, as I mentioned
earlier, we have pods,

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:12.920
and we have persistent
volume claims,

00:35:12.920 --> 00:35:14.420
and then we have
persistent volumes.

00:35:14.420 --> 00:35:16.550
The persistent claim and
the persistent volume

00:35:16.550 --> 00:35:19.370
is still bound together, right?

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:21.140
The pod referenced the claim.

00:35:21.140 --> 00:35:22.557
But if the pod is
gone, then fine.

00:35:22.557 --> 00:35:23.973
There's no reference
to the claim.

00:35:23.973 --> 00:35:26.090
But the claim itself is
still tied to the volume.

00:35:26.090 --> 00:35:27.429
So the data doesn't go away.

00:35:27.429 --> 00:35:28.970
And the claim is
still sitting there.

00:35:28.970 --> 00:35:33.490
We just don't have any pods that
are using that claim, right?

00:35:33.490 --> 00:35:35.410
So that's really
what this illustrates

00:35:35.410 --> 00:35:38.200
is that the pod may go away,
but the persistent volume claim

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:39.884
is still sitting there.

00:35:39.884 --> 00:35:41.800
It's still bound to the
same persistent volume

00:35:41.800 --> 00:35:42.640
that we had before.

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:46.180
And the data is still
not lost, right?

00:35:46.180 --> 00:35:49.150
And then let's say a user
creates a new pod again,

00:35:49.150 --> 00:35:51.262
and is referencing--
could be another pod,

00:35:51.262 --> 00:35:53.220
could be the same pod
that they bring up again.

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:55.930
As long as it references
that same my PVC, which

00:35:55.930 --> 00:35:58.235
is the PV claim, then it will
attach to that same piece

00:35:58.235 --> 00:35:59.360
of persistent volume again.

00:35:59.360 --> 00:36:01.450
So now we're sort of
back to our happy state

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:05.080
again where we were before,
where the pods were restarted,

00:36:05.080 --> 00:36:07.710
new pods came up, yet the
data has never been lost.

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:09.400
So that's pretty cool.

00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:10.300
All right.

00:36:10.300 --> 00:36:13.840
And that's really the state
that we have here, again, right?

00:36:13.840 --> 00:36:15.610
But let's get a little
bit more fancy now.

00:36:15.610 --> 00:36:16.780
And let's delete the pod.

00:36:16.780 --> 00:36:18.880
So let's say the
user deletes the pod.

00:36:18.880 --> 00:36:21.345
And let's also say the
user deleted the claim.

00:36:21.345 --> 00:36:22.720
Now we have an
issue here, right?

00:36:22.720 --> 00:36:25.580
Because while the
claim itself is gone,

00:36:25.580 --> 00:36:28.630
so now it's no longer bound,
so if we did a kube control get

00:36:28.630 --> 00:36:32.230
PV, that 100-gig volume
that was bound before

00:36:32.230 --> 00:36:33.890
is no longer bound anymore.

00:36:33.890 --> 00:36:37.360
So the data itself at this
point is actually deleted.

00:36:37.360 --> 00:36:40.270
So there are a couple of ways
that we can reclaim data here.

00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:41.860
The default way
is we recycle it.

00:36:41.860 --> 00:36:44.150
I mean, essentially, behind this
scenes, like it does an RM dash

00:36:44.150 --> 00:36:45.316
RF of all the data in there.

00:36:45.316 --> 00:36:47.290
So the data is gone, right?

00:36:47.290 --> 00:36:49.480
From that perspective.

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:52.597
And then it's going to be made
available to other claims that

00:36:52.597 --> 00:36:53.680
decide to use that, right?

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:55.870
So that's essentially what
happens to that volume,

00:36:55.870 --> 00:36:59.010
in that sense.

00:36:59.010 --> 00:37:01.020
So that's really kind
of the static case

00:37:01.020 --> 00:37:03.390
that we had with
static provisioning

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:06.240
of persistent volumes and
persistent volume claims.

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:08.360
Now, I did mention
earlier that, you know, we

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:09.610
have dynamic provision, right?

00:37:09.610 --> 00:37:12.930
So it's pretty cool.

00:37:12.930 --> 00:37:14.190
It's late-breaking type stuff.

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:16.440
I mean, this is stuff that's
coming out in Kubernetes.

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:19.320
It's generally available,
you know, in a few weeks.

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:21.240
And you know, and
like I said earlier,

00:37:21.240 --> 00:37:22.820
like living in the
world of cloud,

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:25.960
like we shouldn't have to
pre-provision volumes anymore,

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:26.460
right?

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:29.130
I mean, that's something that
maybe we had to do years ago,

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:30.990
where, you know, you had
someone that was setting up

00:37:30.990 --> 00:37:33.448
these persistent volumes saying,
somebody might come along,

00:37:33.448 --> 00:37:36.390
and they might need
10, 100, 500 gigs,

00:37:36.390 --> 00:37:38.510
and I'm just going to
allocate a bunch them

00:37:38.510 --> 00:37:39.890
available for my users to use.

00:37:39.890 --> 00:37:42.060
Well, rather than
having that paradigm,

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:43.641
like we should be
able to go through,

00:37:43.641 --> 00:37:45.390
and it should be all
on demand now, right?

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:47.490
Like if a user says
whatever they need,

00:37:47.490 --> 00:37:51.000
you know, should be able to make
API calls to a cloud storage

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:53.130
type of provider, and
dynamically, you know,

00:37:53.130 --> 00:37:56.370
build a volume, and then
attach to that person.

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:57.954
And this isn't only cloud.

00:37:57.954 --> 00:37:59.370
It can work within
an organization

00:37:59.370 --> 00:38:00.203
that uses NSF mount.

00:38:00.203 --> 00:38:05.760
It's like you might have storage
available that you can then

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:07.680
create the right amount
of storage available,

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:08.850
and then mount it for them.

00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:11.920
Like that's fine, too, even if
you're not within Cloud, right?

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:15.729
But you shouldn't have to
pre-provision everything just

00:38:15.729 --> 00:38:17.770
in the chance that someone's
going to ask for it.

00:38:17.770 --> 00:38:20.570
So that's really what
dynamic provisioning is here

00:38:20.570 --> 00:38:22.924
to address.

00:38:22.924 --> 00:38:24.840
Now, the key concept
with dynamic provisioning

00:38:24.840 --> 00:38:27.131
is that it uses what's called
the storage class, right?

00:38:27.131 --> 00:38:30.960
So in the example here we set up
two different storage classes.

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:34.170
We set up a storage class for
slow, which is essentially

00:38:34.170 --> 00:38:36.180
it's a sort of regular disk.

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:37.701
It's PV standard.

00:38:37.701 --> 00:38:39.450
And in the case here,
with the provisioner

00:38:39.450 --> 00:38:42.760
is we set it to be
Kubernetes IO, GCEPD.

00:38:42.760 --> 00:38:44.760
So it's a Google Compute
Engine Persistent Disk.

00:38:47.290 --> 00:38:49.424
The parameter
section in this file,

00:38:49.424 --> 00:38:51.090
essentially, it's
just like a large sort

00:38:51.090 --> 00:38:53.830
of blob name, value per
blob, that we just pass on

00:38:53.830 --> 00:38:55.080
to whoever the provisioner is.

00:38:55.080 --> 00:38:58.470
So essentially, the reason
why that's important

00:38:58.470 --> 00:39:01.080
is because whether that's
a Google Compute Engine

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:03.090
Persistent Disk, or
any other provider,

00:39:03.090 --> 00:39:05.400
that parameters section,
you can put whatever

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:07.320
you want in there
depending on whatever

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:09.250
the provider is expecting.

00:39:09.250 --> 00:39:11.657
So if the provider decides
to update their parameters

00:39:11.657 --> 00:39:13.740
with some new stuff, you
can just add it in there.

00:39:13.740 --> 00:39:15.130
And it automatically
will be updated.

00:39:15.130 --> 00:39:16.671
There's no need to
update Kubernetes,

00:39:16.671 --> 00:39:17.880
or anything like that.

00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:20.220
And in this case, we set
up a slower one for that.

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:22.850
And then we have an SSD on
the bottom, which is fast.

00:39:22.850 --> 00:39:25.080
You know, so essentially
the cluster administrator--

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:27.163
and like I said earlier,
the cluster administrator

00:39:27.163 --> 00:39:28.747
and the user might
be the same person.

00:39:28.747 --> 00:39:30.954
There's no necessarily need
for them to be different.

00:39:30.954 --> 00:39:33.150
But in the example here
they're different roles.

00:39:33.150 --> 00:39:35.900
So in this case they've
set up a slow and a fast.

00:39:35.900 --> 00:39:39.450
And to the users they're telling
them, hey, if you want storage

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:42.240
and you want to dynamically
provision it, tell me.

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:46.000
If you want slow storage,
or fast storage, right?

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:48.000
And it depends on what
it is you need, right?

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:51.360
Maybe if it's like some logging
of all the traffic that's

00:39:51.360 --> 00:39:53.250
coming in, slow is fine, right?

00:39:53.250 --> 00:39:56.042
But if you want something that
is going to react a lot faster

00:39:56.042 --> 00:39:57.750
where performance is
much more important,

00:39:57.750 --> 00:39:59.291
maybe you want
something that's fast.

00:39:59.291 --> 00:40:02.220
But you leave it up to the
developer to choose that,

00:40:02.220 --> 00:40:04.230
right?

00:40:04.230 --> 00:40:06.770
So that's what that looks like.

00:40:06.770 --> 00:40:09.050
And with dynamic
provisioning the users

00:40:09.050 --> 00:40:13.040
now consume storage the same
way that they did before.

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:16.380
But instead of in the
claim saying, well,

00:40:16.380 --> 00:40:18.260
just how big it is,
we had this new thing

00:40:18.260 --> 00:40:20.270
added that says
storage class name.

00:40:20.270 --> 00:40:21.950
And here we said fast, right?

00:40:21.950 --> 00:40:25.520
So if we don't have
that storage class name,

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:30.110
then it'll just look for any
existing statically assignable

00:40:30.110 --> 00:40:31.141
persistent volumes.

00:40:31.141 --> 00:40:33.140
But if we have that storage
class name in there,

00:40:33.140 --> 00:40:36.580
then it's going to look for this
in the storage class portion

00:40:36.580 --> 00:40:39.270
that we looked at earlier,
to make sure that's there.

00:40:39.270 --> 00:40:42.770
There was a change made between
Kubernetes version 1.5 and 1.6.

00:40:42.770 --> 00:40:45.270
And that's why it looks a
little bit different there.

00:40:45.270 --> 00:40:47.960
But you know, 1.6
is the new version.

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:49.794
And that's where
we made that change

00:40:49.794 --> 00:40:51.210
to say storage
class name, instead

00:40:51.210 --> 00:40:54.810
of that volume.beta storage
class thing that we had before.

00:40:54.810 --> 00:40:56.930
So that's really how
dynamic provisioning works.

00:40:56.930 --> 00:40:58.971
It's really kind of the
next step into what we're

00:40:58.971 --> 00:41:01.670
doing with volumes.

00:41:01.670 --> 00:41:05.990
So if this stuff was
pretty interesting to you,

00:41:05.990 --> 00:41:09.485
and I hope it really was, please
get involved in community.

00:41:09.485 --> 00:41:11.120
The Slack channel is up there.

00:41:11.120 --> 00:41:14.372
The GitHub code
repository is over there.

00:41:14.372 --> 00:41:15.080
It's open source.

00:41:15.080 --> 00:41:16.770
So anybody can go into
that code depository.

00:41:16.770 --> 00:41:17.810
You can download Kubernetes.

00:41:17.810 --> 00:41:18.601
You can try it out.

00:41:18.601 --> 00:41:20.970
You can also be a
contributor as well.

00:41:20.970 --> 00:41:23.840
So you know, the Slack
channel especially

00:41:23.840 --> 00:41:26.390
is full of people all day
long talking about Kubernetes.

00:41:26.390 --> 00:41:28.492
So you know, if
that's your interests,

00:41:28.492 --> 00:41:30.450
and there are a lot of
minded folks over there.

00:41:30.450 --> 00:41:33.680
So it's a pretty
interesting group.

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:36.150
Now, also-- there it is.

00:41:36.150 --> 00:41:38.187
So there are a couple
other talks here.

00:41:38.187 --> 00:41:40.020
Hopefully you're signed
up for some of them.

00:41:40.020 --> 00:41:42.900
Right after this
talk, across the hall,

00:41:42.900 --> 00:41:44.821
Kelsey Hightower's
basically taking this talk

00:41:44.821 --> 00:41:45.570
to the next level.

00:41:45.570 --> 00:41:48.450
Like, you know, we talked
about creating a container,

00:41:48.450 --> 00:41:50.096
building it, running it.

00:41:50.096 --> 00:41:51.720
He's talking about,
well what would you

00:41:51.720 --> 00:41:54.090
do if you wanted to run that
container in a production

00:41:54.090 --> 00:41:54.670
environment?

00:41:54.670 --> 00:41:58.300
So he talks a lot about
securing that container.

00:41:58.300 --> 00:42:00.210
Some of the name spaces
that we want to use.

00:42:00.210 --> 00:42:02.209
Liveness checks to make
sure your containers are

00:42:02.209 --> 00:42:03.240
always up and running.

00:42:03.240 --> 00:42:05.070
Things like
auto-scaling your pods

00:42:05.070 --> 00:42:06.802
so that you don't
have to sit there

00:42:06.802 --> 00:42:08.760
if you get a sudden spike
in traffic, you know,

00:42:08.760 --> 00:42:10.680
middle of the night,
you're not getting paged.

00:42:10.680 --> 00:42:12.930
The system's auto-scaling
everything for you, right?

00:42:12.930 --> 00:42:15.900
So he's really talking about
how to take your containers

00:42:15.900 --> 00:42:17.850
from development,
which we talked about,

00:42:17.850 --> 00:42:19.260
into actual production.

00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:22.200
There's also some more about
like Container Engine today,

00:42:22.200 --> 00:42:24.029
as well as tomorrow as well.

00:42:24.029 --> 00:42:26.070
I think I talked about
this a little bit earlier.

00:42:26.070 --> 00:42:28.290
But upstairs on the third
floor we have Whack-A-Pod.

00:42:28.290 --> 00:42:30.120
We also have Meet
the Experts up there.

00:42:30.120 --> 00:42:31.200
If you have any
further questions,

00:42:31.200 --> 00:42:32.990
can also talk to me
at any time as well.

00:42:32.990 --> 00:42:34.770
Again, my name is Dan Paik.

00:42:34.770 --> 00:42:36.800
And I'm one of the product
managers on the team.

00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:38.258
And Whack-A-Pod's
a pretty fun game

00:42:38.258 --> 00:42:40.590
you can play up there, where
we have nine pods running.

00:42:40.590 --> 00:42:42.830
As you start whacking
each one the pods go down.

00:42:42.830 --> 00:42:44.290
We basically delete the pod.

00:42:44.290 --> 00:42:46.870
But Kubernetes will keep
bringing them back up again.

00:42:46.870 --> 00:42:49.680
So you can see that,
you know, it's possible

00:42:49.680 --> 00:42:53.820
if you're really fast to
bring your application down.

00:42:53.820 --> 00:42:56.810
But it's usually not down
for more than a second or so

00:42:56.810 --> 00:42:58.480
as it keeps coming up.

00:42:58.480 --> 00:43:01.200
And the Node.js code app that
we went over earlier today

00:43:01.200 --> 00:43:02.669
at the first part
of this, the link

00:43:02.669 --> 00:43:04.710
is over there if you want
to take a look at that.

00:43:04.710 --> 00:43:06.501
If you wanted to run
that yourself at home,

00:43:06.501 --> 00:43:09.300
and play with it in a Container
Engine you can always do that.

00:43:09.300 --> 00:43:15.010
It's free to do if you wanted
to play around with that.

00:43:15.010 --> 00:43:17.760
So that's really kind of what I
wanted to talk to you all about

00:43:17.760 --> 00:43:18.390
today.

00:43:18.390 --> 00:43:20.310
I wanted to make sure I
left about 15 minutes or so

00:43:20.310 --> 00:43:20.893
for questions.

00:43:20.893 --> 00:43:24.670
So if there are any questions
I'm always happy to take them.

00:43:24.670 --> 00:43:26.330
Hopefully I can answer them.

00:43:26.330 --> 00:43:28.890
But yeah, so there
are two mics out there

00:43:28.890 --> 00:43:29.910
if anyone has questions.

00:43:29.910 --> 00:43:32.451
Or if not, you can always feel
free to walk up and talk to me

00:43:32.451 --> 00:43:33.090
as well.

00:43:33.090 --> 00:43:35.040
Thank you very much.

